Ultraviolet (UV) nonlinear optical (NLO) materials capable of expanding the wavelength of solid state lasers to the UV and even deep-UV regions by cascading second harmonic generation (SHG) are important for a variety of significant applications such as laser medical treatment, material micromachining, and photolithography.1-5 However, for UV NLO crystals, they must satisfy the following strict property criteria: (i) wide transparency window in the UV region $\lambda<300~\mathrm{nm}$ , $\mathrm{Eg}>4.2\ \mathrm{eV},$ ), (ii) large SHG coefficient $(>\ d_{36}(\mathrm{KDP})\ =\ 0.39\ \mathrm{\pm/V})$ , (iii) moderate birefringence $(\Delta n\sim0.05\ –0.10)$ to ensure index phase-matching in the UV or even deep-UV regions.6,7 Generally, these requirements cannot be met simultaneously because of their mutually exclusive nature, i.e., SHG coefficients are inversely proportional to the band $\mathrm{gap^{8}}$ - the larger the band gap; the smaller the SHG efficiency. Also, the band gap is often inversely proportional to birefringence.7,8 Therefore, it remains a challenge to balance the above contraindicated requirements, as such the discovery of new UV NLO materials 9–13 are required.
Metal selenites and tellurites are more likely to form noncentrosymmetric (NCS) structures - a structural prerequisite for SHG - because ${\mathrm{Se^{4+}}}$ and $\mathrm{Te^{4+}}$ cations are susceptible to secondorder Jahn–Teller (SOJT) distortions originating from their stereochemically active lone pairs (SCALP).3,14,15 Over the last thirty years , a large number of NCS selenites and tellurites with excellent SHG intensities have been discovered, such as ${\beta{\mathrm{-BaTeMo}_{2}}{\mathrm{O}}_{9}}$ $30\times\mathrm{KDP}$ , $2.95\mathrm{eV})$ ,16,17 $\mathrm{Ag_{2}M o_{3}T e_{3}O_{16}}$ (8 $\times\ \mathrm{KDP}$ , $2.85\ \mathrm{eV}$ ),18 $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{TeMo}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{12}$ $7\times\mathrm{KDP}$ , $2.88\ \mathrm{eV}$ ),14 and ${\mathrm{BiFSeO}}_{3}$ ( $13.5\times\mathrm{KDP}$ , $3.83\ \mathrm{eV}$ ).15  Nonetheless, the majority of them have small band gaps $\mathrm{(E_{g}\sim4.2e V)}$ that hinder their applications in shorter wavelength ranges, i.e. the UV and deep-UV region.19
In this study, we have noticed that band gaps of perovskite materials in solar cells can be effectively regulated by changing the thickness of the absorption layers. For example, in (A′) $(\mathrm{MA})_{\mathrm{n-1}}\mathrm{Pb}_{\mathrm{n}}\mathrm{I}_{3\mathrm{n}+1}$ ( $\mathbf{A}^{\prime}=3\mathbf{AMP}$ or 4AMP, ${\mathfrak{n}}=1{-}4$ ) Dion−Jacobson perovskites, the optical band gaps of both 3AMP and 4AMP series follow a general trend that the energy gap $(E_{g})$ decreases as the layer number (n) increases. From $\mathbf{n}=1$ to $\mathtt{n}=4$ $(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3})$ , the band gaps of 3AMP series decrease from 2.23 to $1.87\mathrm{eV}$ , and decrease from 2.38 to $1.89~\mathrm{eV}$ for the 4AMP series.20 Clearly, in these perovskite materials of solar cells, the thinner the absorption layers, the larger the band gap. This has been well described as the band gap engineering in perovskite solar cell materials.20 Likely, Aurivillius structures with a general formula $\mathrm{(Bi_{2}O_{2})(A_{n-1}B_{n}O_{3n+1})^{21}}$ have similar structure features with the perovskite materials of solar cells, i.e. the $\mathrm{\nABO}_{3}$ $\left({\mathfrak{n}}\ =\ 1-7\right)$ perovskite octahedral layers are separated by the $\mathrm{[Bi_{2}O_{2}]^{2+}}_{\infty}$ layers. Therefore, the same band gap engineering can also be used in Aurivillius structures.
Using these ideas, we attempted to introduce Aurivillius-type structures into tellurites in order to adjust their band gaps for applications as UV or deep-UV NLO materials. Initially, we used $\mathrm{[TeF_{2}(O H)_{2}]_{\infty}}$ layers to substitute the $\mathrm{\nABO}_{3}$ $(\mathrm{n}=1-7)$ ) perovskite layers of the Aurivillius structure. Considering the layer numbers are inversely proportional to the band gaps, we prefer $[\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}]_{\infty}$ single-layer configuration. Second, we substitute the $\mathrm{[Bi_{2}O_{2}]^{2+}}_{\infty}$ layers by using neutral $[\mathrm{BaF}_{2}]_{\infty}$ layers, in which the $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ cations have no d-d or f-f electron transitions and $\operatorname{F}^{-}$ anions will blue-shift the absorption edge, such as ${\mathrm{BaBOF}}_{3}$ $(7.01~\mathrm{{~eV})}$ 22 and $\mathrm{BaClBF}_{4}(6.55~\mathrm{~eV})$ .23 In doing the above, we successfully synthesized a pseudo-Aurivillius-type tellurite, $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ by a straightforward hydrothermal reaction. Remarkably, the Te-F bonds are observed in its structure, so it belongs to a fluorotellurite. For fluorotellurites, only $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Te}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{F}_{4},{}^{24}$ $\mathrm{HgTeO}_{2}\mathrm{F}(\mathrm{OH}),{^{25}}$ $\mathrm{MTeO(OH)F_{4}(M=K}$ , Rb),26 $\mathrm{TeOF}_{2}{}^{27}$ and $\mathrm{KTeOF}_{3}{}^{28}$ have NCS structures, but none of their NLO properties have been reported. Therefore, $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is the first NLO active fluorotellurite material. Herein, we report on its synthesis, crystal structure, optical and NLO properties.
$\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ was synthesized through hydrothermal methods. Powder X-ray diffraction measurement shows the resulted product is $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ (Figure S1), indicating $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is the only product of the reaction, and the yield can reach more than $95\%$ based on $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}$ . The IR spectra is shown in Figure S2. The bands associated with the Te–O vibrations appeared at 874 and $500\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The bands associated with the Te–F vibrations appeared at $683.6~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ .29 The energy dispersive $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray spectroscope (EDX) indicates that the molar ratio of Ba: Te: F: O is 1.28: 1.18: 4.85: 2.17, which is approximately equal to the theoretical one, 1: 1: 4: 2 (Figure S3).
The crystal structure was determined through single crystal Xray diffraction. It crystallizes in a NCS and polar orthorhombic space group, $P m n2_{1}$ (No. 31), with the lattice parameters $a$ $=7.501\bar{3}(4)\bar{\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ , $b=8.6517(4)\AA$ Å and $c=4.2142(2)\mathrm{~\AA~}$ (Table S1). In the asymmetric unit, there are one unique Ba atom, one unique Te atom, one unique O atom, and three unique F atoms (Table S2). The structure is shown in Figure 1. The $\mathrm{Ba}^{2+}$ cations are coordi
nated to eight fluorine atoms and form $\mathrm{BaF_{8}}$ polyhedra. Each BaF8 polyhedron connects with adjacent six $\mathrm{BaF_{8}}$ polyhedra through edge-sharing to form the corrugated $[\mathrm{BaF}_{2}]_{\infty}$ layers that extend in the $a c$ -plane (Figure 1a). The $\mathrm{Te^{4+}}$ cations are coordinated by two oxygen atoms and two fluorine atoms and form a distorted ${\mathrm{TeF}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ polyhedron. All of the oxygen and fluoride ions fall within the same hemisphere around the $\mathrm{Te^{4+}}$ cation, indicating that the lone pairs on $\mathrm{Te^{4+}}$ cations are stereochemically active. The ${\mathrm{TeF}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ polyhedra are also connected by hydrogen bonds through O1 atoms (O1-H…O1) to form a $\mathrm{[TeF_{2}(O H)_{2}]_{\infty}}$ layer (Figure 1b). In the structure, the $\mathrm{[TeF_{2}(O H)_{2}]_{\infty}}$ layers and $[\mathrm{BaF}_{2}]_{\infty}$ layers stack alternatively along the $c$ -axis to form the three-dimentional framework of $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ (Figure 1c). Clearly, the structure of $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is similiar to the Aurivillius structure. It can be seen as the $[\mathrm{Bi}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}]^{2+}\infty$ layers in Aurivillius structure are substituted by the $[\mathrm{BaF}_{2}]_{\infty}$ layers whereas the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{nABO}}_{3}$ $(\mathrm{n}=1-7)$ layers are substituted by the $[\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}]_{\infty}$ layers (Figure 1d).
For the ${\mathrm{TeF}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ polyhedra, they are extremely distorted becasue of the SCALP of $\mathrm{Te^{4+}}$ cations. The Te-F bond lengths range from 1.971(6) to 2.050(6) Å and the two Te-O bond lengths are identical (1.888(5) Å). For $\mathrm{BaF_{8}}$ polyhedra, the Ba-F bond lengths range from 2.658(4) to 2.839(6) Å (Table S3). All of the bond lengths are consistent with those reported earlier.19,27 Bond valence calculations30 result in bond valance sums (BVS) is consistent with their oxidation states (Table S2).
In order to determine the transimission window of $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , a plate-shaped crystal with size of $2.0\times1.8\times0.6$ $\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ was used for UV-vis-NIR transmittance spectra (Figures 2) and IR transmission measurements (Figures S4). $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ exhibits a wide transparent region from 0.2 to $8.3\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ , with an absorption around $3\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ from the hydroxyl group. Clearly, the transmission window of $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is wider than other reported NCS metal tellurites and tellurates (Table 1). In particular, $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ has a short UV absorption edge that will favor its application as UV NLO material. The  short UV absorption edge can be attributed to its unique pseudo-Aurivillius structure. Firstly, the $[\mathrm{BaF}_{2}]_{\infty}$ layers are transparent in the UV region. Secondly, the $\operatorname{F}^{}$ anions with strongly electronegativity have been introduced into tellurite. It is well known that the $\operatorname{F}^{-}$ anions will be helpful for the enhancement of the band gaps of materials.10,13,23 In addition, the single-layer configration of $\mathrm{[TeF_{2}(O H)_{2}]_{\infty}}$ will also be conducive for wide UV transmision based on the band gap engineering.
Furthermore, the powder SHG response of $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ was measured by the Kurtz-Perry method.42 The SHG intensity increases as the particle size increases, indicating that $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is type-I phase-matchable (Figure 3a). The SHG intensity comparison between $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ and commercial KDP shows that the SHG intensity is about $3\times\mathrm{KDP}$ (Figure 3b). In order to better understand the origin of SHG response of $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , a dipole moment analysis based on the bond valence method43 has been carried out. In the calculations, the lone pairs of the $\mathrm{Te^{4+}}$ cations are given a charge of -2 and localized
1.25 Å from the $\mathrm{Te^{4+}}$ cation.44 As listed in Table S4, ${\mathrm{TeF}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ polyhedron has a large distortion and produces a dipole moment of 10.26 Debye, which is comparable with other tellurites, such as $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{3}\mathrm{TeO}_{12}{}^{32}$ and $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{TeO}_{12}$ .17 More importantly, the dipole moments of ${\mathrm{TeF}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ polyhedra adapt an additive arrangement manner toward the $c$ -axis in a unit cell (Figure S5) that results in a very large net dipole moment -19.94 Debye. For polar materials, a large net dipole moment in a unit cell often corresponds to a large SHG response.9,11 Therefore, the large SHG response of $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ can be attributed to the large distortion of ${\mathrm{TeF}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ polyhedra and their additive dipole moments.
Apart from wide transmission range and large SHG response, a suitable birefringence is also necessary for a UV NLO material to achieve index phase-matching behavior.9-13 The birefringence of $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ was measured using a cross-polarizing microscope.45 Based on the crystal optics, the birefringence, thickness of sample, and retardation follow the equation
$$
R={\varDelta}n\times d
$$
where $R,\Delta n$ and $d$ are retardation, birefringence, and thickness, respectively.46 Therefore, a plate-shaped $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ crystal with a thickness of $27\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure S6) was used for the birefringence measurement. The observed interference color in cross-polarized light was the fourth order pink (Figure 4a). Matching with the Michal–Levy chart, one can obtain the retardation of about $2100\mathrm{nm}$ .46 Further, the birefringence of 0.078 can be calculated by using the above equation. Remarkably, the birefringence measured by the above method is a birefringence inplane of the measured crystal, not the largest birefringence. The largest birefringence of $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ should be equal to or larger than 0.078, which indicates that $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ will have excellent phase-matching capabilities in the UV and deep-UV region.
In addition, the thermal stability and environment stability of $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ are also measured. The TG/DSC curves are shown in Figure S7a. On the DSC curve, an endothermic peak around 220 $^\mathrm{{{o}C}}$ is observed, accompanied by $10.6\%$ weight loss. The powder XRD measurement shows that the residual after  TG/DSC is the mixture of ${\mathrm{TeO}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}$ (Figure S7b), indicating that the weight loss at $220~^{\mathrm{oC}}$ is caused by the loss of two molecules HF, with calculated weight loss of $10.3\%$ . A transparent and inclusion-free $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ single crystal was also placed in the water for a moisture-resistance test (Figure 4b). It is clear that no any decomposition or degradation has been observed after soaking it in water for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , indicating that $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ has good environmental stability that will favor its practical applications.
To summarize, a new NCS and polar fluorotellurite, $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ , has been successfully synthesized. Its structure can be seen as an interesting pseudo-Aurivillius structure. In the structure, the UV-transparent $[\mathrm{BaF}_{2}]_{\infty}$ layers and the $\mathrm{[TeF_{2}(O H)_{2}]_{\infty}}$ single-layers substitute for the $\mathrm{[Bi_{2}O_{2}]^{2+}}_{\infty}$ layers and the $\mathrm{nABO}_{3}$ (n $\begin{array}{r l}{=}&{{}1-7^{\cdot}}\end{array}$ perovskite layers respectively. Based on band gap engineering of perovskite solar cells, the single-layer
$\mathrm{[TeF_{2}(O H)_{2}]_{\infty}}$ configration will be conducive to generating a large band gap. Therefore, $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ can achieve a desired balance between a wide band gap, large SHG response, and suitable birefringence, i.e. $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ has a short UV absorption edge $(\sim205~\mathrm{nm})$ , large SHG response $(\sim3\ \times\ \mathrm{KDP})$ ) and moderate birefringence $(\sim0.078@350{\sim}700\ \mathrm{nm})$ . These properties suggest that $\mathrm{BaF}_{2}\mathrm{TeF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ is a promising UV NLO material. The exploration of other pseudo-Aurivillius structures based on band gap engineering is ongoing.
